::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source set: A student is researching whether pre-order lunch forms can reduce unwanted trays. Which research question is most focused?{
~A. What is lunch?#Incorrect. This question is too broad and not useful for research.
=B. How can student pre-order lunch forms help reduce unwanted trays?#Correct. This question is specific, researchable, and aligned to the unit topic.
~C. Why do schools exist?#Incorrect. This does not match the topic.
~D. Are forms always perfect?#Incorrect. This is too broad and absolute.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source: A district pilot report includes data about trays before and after a pre-order program. Why is this source relevant?{
~A. It has a long title.#Incorrect. A title length does not prove relevance.
~B. It uses many pages.#Incorrect. Length does not guarantee relevance.
~C. It is about an unrelated sport.#Incorrect. An unrelated sport would not answer the question.
=D. It provides evidence directly connected to the research question.#Correct. Relevant sources help answer the research question.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: "Reminders helped more students complete the pre-order form after the first week." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
=A. After the first week, reminders increased how many students finished the form.#Correct. This restates the meaning accurately.
~B. Reminders made forms unnecessary.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~C. Students never completed forms.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
~D. Forms were canceled after reminders.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains evidence?{
~A. Evidence is evidence.#Incorrect. This does not explain meaning.
~B. The report had information.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=C. The finding matters because student selections helped staff prepare meals students were more likely to choose.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~D. Lunch forms are forms.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits a student audience?{
=A. Completing the form helps the cafeteria prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This is clear and audience-aware.
~B. Pre-order compliance improves operational outcomes.#Incorrect. This is too formal.
~C. The form thing is good.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. Submit documentation for food-service optimization.#Incorrect. This is too formal and unclear.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual best supports a publication about reduced unwanted trays?{
~A. A random hallway picture.#Incorrect. This does not support the message.
~B. A decorative border only.#Incorrect. Decoration does not explain evidence.
~C. A picture of unrelated school supplies.#Incorrect. This visual is unrelated.
=D. A labeled before-and-after chart showing unwanted trays before and after the pilot.#Correct. This visual directly supports the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is complete and clear?{
~A. Because the report found fewer unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=B. The report found fewer unwanted trays after the pre-order pilot.#Correct. This is a complete sentence.
~C. Report trays students because.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~D. When forms lunch.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which group role best supports a presentation about a research-based publication?{
~A. One person speaks while no one else prepares.#Incorrect. This does not show collaboration.
~B. Everyone repeats the same sentence.#Incorrect. Repetition weakens communication.
=C. One member explains evidence, one explains wording, one explains the visual, and one explains revision choices.#Correct. These roles support clear collaborative presentation.
~D. The group skips explaining the source.#Incorrect. The audience needs the source evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence uses an appropriate academic voice?{
=A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays when staff used student selections.#Correct. This is precise and evidence-based.
~B. The lunch thing was awesome.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~C. Students better do the form.#Incorrect. This is demanding rather than explanatory.
~D. The report said stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U04_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which sentence best synthesizes two source findings?{
~A. The sources are both about lunch.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~B. One source exists and another source exists.#Incorrect. This does not combine ideas.
=C. The pilot report shows fewer unwanted trays, while the manager's comment shows reminders helped students complete the forms consistently.#Correct. This combines what each source contributes.
~D. Forms are always easy for everyone.#Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}
